  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254788/assets/e3fbdaa6f3054a4d6e97a33306d7e3e5b0215d03c9f6a148406ac5b950459a2a.png)\n\nThe picture above shows the graph of a function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx).$ Find the value of $B.$",
  "worked_solution": "In order to find the value of $B,$ we first need to find the period of the function.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that function makes a complete cycle on the interval $[0,\\frac{4\\pi }{5}].$ So, the period is $\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254788/assets/ea4729c43c21f5206c9abd5b756efd86707cf5420640af2f86e850eb34109824.png)\n\nWe know that for a function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx),$ the horizontal scaling factor is $\\frac{1}{B},$ and the period is multiplied by the scaling factor:\n\n$$\n\\text{period}=2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}\n$$\n\nFor our particular function, the period is $\\frac{4\\pi }{5},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\pi }{5} & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B} \\\\ B & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
